-**How a limit order is matched.** Every tick says which side started the trade (the `Flag` column of the tick data). `Buy` means a buyer took a seller's offer, so someone was **offering** at that price. `Sell` means a seller hit a buyer's bid, so that price was the **bid**, and the cheapest offer is about one price step higher. "One price step" is 0.25 around 60 baht and 1.00 around 250.
+**How a limit order is matched.** The engine takes the other side's best price to be the last price one **tick size** away (the slippage rule, 1 tick): the offer is `last price + 1 tick`, the bid is `last price − 1 tick`, whatever side the tick was. A tick is 0.25 around 60 baht and 1.00 around 250 — see [Tick size](#tick-size).
 
 | | Buy limit | Sell limit |
 |---|---|---|
-| **When placed** — fills at once if | the offer is at or below your price (`Buy` tick: offer = last price · `Sell` tick: offer = last price + one step) | the bid is at or above your price (`Sell` tick: bid = last price · `Buy` tick: bid = last price − one step) |
-| You pay / receive | the offer — your price or better | the bid — your price or better |
-| **Otherwise it rests**, and fills once | the market trades **below** your price | the market trades **above** your price |
-| You pay / receive | your price | your price |
-| **How many shares** (both cases) | at most the volume of the tick that fills it, in whole lots of 100 | same |
+| Fills if | `last price + 1 tick` is **at or below** your price | `last price − 1 tick` is **at or above** your price |
+| You pay / receive | `last price + 1 tick` — your price or better | `last price − 1 tick` — your price or better |
+| How many shares | at most the tick's volume, in whole lots of 100 | same |
+| What doesn't fill | cancelled at once | cancelled at once |
 
 Fees (commission 0.157% + VAT 7% of it) come on top of every fill.
 
-Example, last price 62.75 for a Buy: on a `Buy` tick a limit at 62.75 fills at once at 62.75; on a `Sell` tick the same order waits; a limit at 63.50 fills at once on either tick, at 62.75 or 63.00 respectively — never at 63.50.
-
-**Why a resting order waits for a trade *through* its price.** Other people's orders were queued at your price before yours. The ticks show trades, not how long that queue was, so a trade exactly at your price may have gone to them. A trade beyond your price means the queue at your price was used up — yours included. Example: a Buy resting at 2.94 does not fill while the market trades at 2.94, however much trades there; the first trade at 2.92 fills it, at 2.94.
-
-- **Partial fills.** Each fill is at most the volume of the tick that filled it, rounded down to whole lots of 100 (a tick of fewer than 100 shares fills nothing). The rest of the order keeps resting; [`get_open_orders()`](#managing-resting-orders) shows how much is still waiting. When several of your orders fill on one tick, they share its volume in the order you placed them.
-- **Auction ticks** (`OPEN1_E`, `OPEN2_E`, `ATC`) have no side and never fill a limit order.
-- **A resting order lasts for the trading day.** Each day starts with an empty order book; whatever is still waiting at the close is gone — a whole order, or the rest of one that filled in part. Place it again the next day if you still want it.
-- **Orders fill in the order you placed them.** When the price arrives, the engine checks your cash (Buy) or holdings (Sell) again. If they are no longer enough — for example you already spent the cash, or two Sell orders wait on the same 100 shares — that order is cancelled.
-- **Every call places a new order.** A strategy that calls `create_order_to_limit` on every tick while a condition holds piles up resting orders, and they can all fill later. Check [`get_open_orders()`](#managing-resting-orders) first, or cancel what you no longer want.
-- A limit at `bar["Close"]` is **not** guaranteed to fill — on a `Sell` tick it waits, and then needs a trade through its price. When the strategy has to get in or out now, use a market order.
-- **The day's volume cap.** An order is refused if it asks for more than its stock has traded on the other side so far today, less what your team has already bought (for a Buy) or sold (for a Sell) in that stock today.
-- "created successfully" means the order passed validation (cash, holdings, volume) and is in the order book, not that it filled. Check with [`check_port_has_stock()`](#check_port_has_stocksymbol-volume) or [`get_all_stocks_info()`](#get_all_stocks_info).
-
-### **Create order at the market** Fill right away, whatever side the tick was: a Buy pays the latest price plus one price step, a Sell receives the latest price minus one step (plus fees either way). The step stands in for crossing the spread to the other side's best price. Only for the stock of the current bar. Use it when filling now matters more than the price.
+Example, last price 62.75 (tick 0.25): a Buy limit at 63.00 or higher fills at 63.00; a Buy limit at 62.75 does not fill and is cancelled. A Sell limit at 62.50 or lower fills at 62.50; a Sell limit at 62.75 is cancelled.
+
+- **Only once.** An order for the bar's own stock (`bar["ShareCode"]`) is matched on the current tick. An order for another stock is matched on that stock's next tick. Either way it is then gone, filled or not. A later tick never fills it, so if you still want the trade, place it again.
+- **At most the tick's volume.** A fill is at most the volume of that tick, rounded down to whole lots of 100 (a tick of fewer than 100 shares fills nothing), less what your earlier orders on the same tick already took — market orders included. The rest of the order is cancelled.
+- **The price must be a multiple of the tick size** at that price, in the table of that trading day — e.g. 58.25 is fine at 0.25 steps, 58.10 is rejected. See [Tick size](#tick-size).
+- **Auction ticks** (`OPEN1_E`, `OPEN2_E`, `ATC` — any tick whose `Flag` is not `Buy`/`Sell`) fill nothing.
+- **Cash and holdings are checked again when it fills.** If two Sell orders on one tick both want the same 100 shares, the second is skipped.
+- A limit at `bar["Close"]` does **not** fill: a Buy needs at least `Close + 1 tick`, a Sell at most `Close − 1 tick`.
+- **The day's volume cap.** An order can fill at most what its stock has traded on the other side so far today, less what your team has already bought (for a Buy) or sold (for a Sell) in that stock today, in whole lots of 100. An order bigger than that is still accepted and fills at most what is left (e.g. Buy 100,000 when 50,000 is left → at most 50,000, and no more than the tick's volume); it is refused only when less than 100 shares are left.
+- "created successfully" means the order passed validation (price, cash, holdings, volume), not that it filled. Check with [`check_port_has_stock()`](#check_port_has_stocksymbol-volume) or [`get_all_stocks_info()`](#get_all_stocks_info).
+
+### **Create order at the market** Market-to-Limit IOC: fill now at the other side's best price, whatever side the tick was — a Buy pays the latest price plus one tick, a Sell receives the latest price minus one tick (plus fees either way). The tick stands in for crossing the spread. Like a limit order it gets at most the tick's volume and the rest is cancelled. Only for the stock of the current bar. Use it when filling now matters more than the price.
 
