+### **Tick size (ช่วงราคา)**
+
+The tick size is the smallest price step at a price level. It is the slippage of every fill (1 tick), and a limit price must be a whole multiple of it. SET changes the table on **16 Nov 2026**; the engine uses the table of the trading day being simulated (data: `tradeSim/tick_size.json`).
+
+| Price (baht) | Tick before 16 Nov 2026 | Tick from 16 Nov 2026 |
+| :---- | :---- | :---- |
+| below 2 | 0.01 | 0.01 |
+| 2 – below 5 | 0.02 | 0.02 |
+| 5 – below 10 | 0.05 | 0.02 |
+| 10 – below 25 | 0.10 | 0.05 |
+| 25 – below 50 | 0.25 | 0.10 |
+| 50 – below 100 | 0.25 | 0.25 |
+| 100 – below 200 | 0.50 | 0.50 |
+| 200 – below 400 | 1.00 | 1.00 |
+| 400 and up | 2.00 | 2.00 |
+
+Example: PTT at 42 has a tick of 0.25 until 13 Nov (42.10 is rejected) and 0.10 from 16 Nov (42.10 is fine).
+
+A sell when the last price sits exactly on a band edge steps one tick of the **lower** band, since the next price under it is in that band: last 100.00 → a Sell fills at 99.75 (tick 0.25), not 99.50; a Buy at 100.00 fills at 100.50 (tick 0.50).
 
