-**Partial fills.** A limit order can fill in pieces (each fill is at most the volume of the tick that filled it), so one `Order Number` can appear on several rows — one per fill, each with its own `Volume`, `Market Price` and `Timestamp`. Add up `Volume` across an order's rows to see how much of it filled.
-
-**What never filled.** The log records fills only. Whatever is still resting at the close — a whole order, or the rest of one that filled in part — is cancelled with the day's order book and leaves no row. Example: a Buy for 68,000 with rows of 20,000 and 10,000 filled 30,000; the other 38,000 lapsed at the close. Place it again the next day if you still want it.
+A Buy (market or limit) shows `Price` = `Market Price` + 1 tick, a Sell `Market Price` − 1 tick. A limit Buy's `Price` is at or below its `Order Price`.
+
+**Partial fills.** An order fills at most once, so one `Order Number` has at most one row. Its `Volume` can be less than you asked for — the tick's volume ran out — and the rest was cancelled at once.
+
+**What never filled.** The log records fills only. An order that didn't fill, or the part of one that didn't, leaves no row. Example: a Buy for 68,000 on a tick of 30,000 shares has one row of 30,000; the other 38,000 were cancelled right then. Check what you hold with [`get_all_stocks_info()`](#get_all_stocks_info).
 
